<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<settings version="3.1.1">
  <id>1103f3e9-ac22-41eb-aa4c-64e21f1e6bfd</id>
  <name>Mediator Services Extensions</name>
  <designerReferences />
  <packageSettings />
  <packageExtensions />
  <elementSettings />
  <elementExtensions>
    <elementExtension type="Command" typeId="ccf14eb6-3a55-4d81-b5b9-d27311c70cb9">
      <mappingSettings />
      <rules>In this application, the handler for this Command is written by an AI agent, never by the Software Factory (Intent.Application.Mediator). The Software Factory generates only a stub whose Handle body throws NotImplementedException. The associations modelled on this Command (Create Entity, Update Entity, Delete Entity, Query Entity, Invoke, Publish Integration Event, Send Integration Command and so on) are the specification for that body, in model order. Field mappings are not modelled. Whenever you create this Command, or add, remove, reorder or change any association on it, find its handler once the Software Factory has generated it and write or update the handler body in the same piece of work, using the mediator-command-handler skill. Nothing else will prompt you: a new stub raises no task, and a changed association changes no generated file.</rules>
    </elementExtension>
    <elementExtension type="Integration Event Handler" typeId="c0582230-22f5-4f74-8eb0-ec6fc9364900">
      <mappingSettings />
      <rules>In this application, integration event handlers are generated by Eventing.Contracts and finished by an AI agent (Intent.Application.Mediator). The subscriptions and actions modelled on this Integration Event Handler (Invoke Command, Publish Integration Event, Send Integration Command and so on) are the specification for each HandleAsync body, in model order. Field mappings are not modelled. Eventing.Contracts silently skips an Invoke and generates a Publish or Send without field mappings as an empty message, so a handler with an Invoke, or with a Publish or Send that has no field mappings, must be finished by hand: take HandleAsync over with Body = Mode.Ignore and write the whole body. Whenever you create this handler, or add, remove, reorder or change any of its subscriptions or actions, check its HandleAsync bodies once the Software Factory has generated them and finish or update them in the same piece of work, using the mediator-integration-event-handler skill. Nothing else will prompt you: no task is raised, and a changed action may change no generated file.</rules>
    </elementExtension>
    <elementExtension type="Query" typeId="e71b0662-e29d-4db2-868b-8a12464b25d0">
      <mappingSettings />
      <rules>In this application, the handler for this Query is written by an AI agent, never by the Software Factory (Intent.Application.Mediator). The Software Factory generates only a stub whose Handle body throws NotImplementedException. The associations modelled on this Query (Create Entity, Update Entity, Delete Entity, Query Entity, Invoke, Publish Integration Event, Send Integration Command and so on) are the specification for that body, in model order. Field mappings are not modelled. Whenever you create this Query, or add, remove, reorder or change any association on it, find its handler once the Software Factory has generated it and write or update the handler body in the same piece of work, using the mediator-query-handler skill. Nothing else will prompt you: a new stub raises no task, and a changed association changes no generated file.</rules>
    </elementExtension>
  </elementExtensions>
  <associationSettings />
  <associationExtensions />
  <mappingSettings />
  <mappableElementPackages />
  <mappableElementPackageExtensions />
  <scripts />
</settings>